{"page":7445,"revision":1371729148,"ref":"o5","kind":"section","cue":"section","strength":"strong","name":"Statement of the classification theorem","section":"Statement of the classification theorem","section_anchor":"Statement_of_the_classification_theorem","span":[2319,3736],"citations":["o8"],"hash":"sha256:232f2c1dc165bd8f523d730c2b64c012c8d9e0c90663908e08e492c084fafc4a","extractor":"m2/v0.3.8"}
{"page":7445,"revision":1371729148,"ref":"o6","kind":"statement/theorem","cue":"named-heading","strength":"normal","name":"Statement of the classification theorem","section":"Statement of the classification theorem","section_anchor":"Statement_of_the_classification_theorem","span":[2319,3736],"text":"## Statement of the classification theorem\nEvery finite simple group is, up to isomorphism, one of the following groups:\n* a member of 18 specific infinite families of simple groups, namely,\n** cyclic groups of prime order,\n** alternating groups of degree at least 5,\n** 16 other infinite families known as the simple groups of Lie type,\n* or one of 26 specific groups called the \"sporadic groups\".\nThe classification theorem has applications in many branches of mathematics, as questions about the structure of finite groups (and their action on other mathematical objects) can sometimes be reduced to questions about finite simple groups. Thanks to the classification theorem, such questions can sometimes be answered by checking each family of simple groups and each sporadic group.\nDaniel Gorenstein announced in 1983 that the finite simple groups had all been classified, but this was premature as he had been misinformed about the proof of the classification of quasithin groups. The completed proof of the classification was announced by after Aschbacher and Smith published a 1221-page proof for the missing quasithin case.","links":[{"title":"Simple group"},{"title":"Isomorphism"},{"title":"Cyclic group"},{"title":"Alternating groups"},{"title":"List of finite simple groups","anchor":"Groups_of_Lie_type"},{"title":"Sporadic groups"},{"title":"Finite group"},{"title":"Daniel Gorenstein"},{"title":"Quasithin group"}],"citations":["o8"],"hash":"sha256:232f2c1dc165bd8f523d730c2b64c012c8d9e0c90663908e08e492c084fafc4a","extractor":"m2/v0.3.8"}
{"page":7445,"revision":1371729148,"ref":"o7","kind":"statement/theorem","cue":"math-theorem","strength":"strong","name":"Theorem","section":"Statement of the classification theorem","section_anchor":"Statement_of_the_classification_theorem","span":[2416,2847],"text":"Every finite simple group is, up to isomorphism, one of the following groups:\n* a member of 18 specific infinite families of simple groups, namely,\n** cyclic groups of prime order,\n** alternating groups of degree at least 5,\n** 16 other infinite families known as the simple groups of Lie type,\n* or one of 26 specific groups called the \"sporadic groups\".","links":[{"title":"Simple group"},{"title":"Isomorphism"},{"title":"Cyclic group"},{"title":"Alternating groups"},{"title":"List of finite simple groups","anchor":"Groups_of_Lie_type"},{"title":"Sporadic groups"}],"hash":"sha256:b086b75a29c7d56f425ab9ac145cc7d91d9b24b3522914675f7234763ce9705a","extractor":"m2/v0.3.8"}
